Subject: [PATCH] device: drain an autorelease pool around every C ABI entry

Every call into this device from QEMU runs on one of QEMU's threads, and none
of them has an autorelease pool that is ever drained. Every object Metal hands
back autoreleased — command buffers, encoders, descriptors, and the resources
they keep alive — therefore lived for the rest of the process.

On a desktop that is a slow leak nobody hits. On an iPhone, under a 3 GB limit
with 2 GB of it the guest's RAM, it was the whole budget: IOAccelerator memory
went 20 -> 279 MB in the minutes after the login window came up while every
cache this device keeps stayed under 5 MB, and the system killed the app.

`unwind_safe` is the one function every entry runs through, so the pool goes
there, on Apple hosts only.

diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/device/mod.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/device/mod.rs
index 5be4a40a..f1efd0ab 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/device/mod.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/device/mod.rs
@@ -828,6 +828,23 @@ where
     F: FnOnce() -> T + std::panic::UnwindSafe,
 {
     crate::observe::panic::arm();
+    // Every entry drains its own autorelease pool on an Apple host. The threads
+    // that call in are QEMU's, and none of them has a pool that is ever drained,
+    // so every object Metal hands back autoreleased — command buffers, encoders,
+    // descriptors, and whatever they keep alive — outlived its call for the life
+    // of the process. Measured on an iPhone: IOAccelerator memory 20 → 279 MB
+    // in the minutes after the login window came up, with every cache this
+    // device keeps under 5 MB, and the app killed at its memory limit.
+    #[cfg(target_vendor = "apple")]
+    return objc::rc::autoreleasepool(|| run_entry(entry, f, on_panic));
+    #[cfg(not(target_vendor = "apple"))]
+    run_entry(entry, f, on_panic)
+}
+
+fn run_entry<T, F>(entry: &'static str, f: F, on_panic: T) -> T
+where
+    F: FnOnce() -> T + std::panic::UnwindSafe,
+{
     match catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(f)) {
         Ok(v) => v,
         Err(payload) => {
